abstract=f"Life-stage comparisons under ocean warming and acidification depend on the endpoints represented in experiments. We reanalysed {len(d):,} log response ratios from {d.StudyID.nunique()} studies, correcting cross-layer duplication, dose-specific record loss and endpoint transformations. Ecological subgroup analyses preceded comparisons of directional, endpoint-aligned and absolute responses. In metamorphic taxa ({len(primary):,} effects; {primary.StudyID.nunique()} studies), the embryo directional mean was {f(emb.Estimate)} (95% confidence interval {f(emb.CI_low)} to {f(emb.CI_high)}). Source-based alignment retained negative "+' and '.join(labels[s] for s in supported.ObservedStage)+f" means after false-discovery-rate correction. Corrected between-stage tests supported {stage_sig} pairwise differences. On matched evidence, {n} of {len(paired)} numerical target contrasts survived correction; {fold} involved absolute transformation. Directional and aligned responses can therefore agree, while folding changes their interpretation. Settlement/metamorphosis evidence was insufficient for performance inference: {settle_text}. Recovered exposure records allowed driver-specific dose adjustment, and experimental-unit re-extraction reduced individual-level pseudoreplication. Historical screening records and independent review of new endpoint mappings remain incomplete. The results support endpoint-specific interpretation of early-life responses, rather than a universal vulnerability ranking. Recruitment assessment requires comparable exposures and linked measurements of settlement and subsequent survival."
abstract=abstract.replace('correcting cross-layer duplication, dose-specific record loss and endpoint transformations.', 'to examine how ecological context and response definitions shape inferred vulnerability.')
abstract=abstract.replace('Recovered exposure records allowed driver-specific dose adjustment, and experimental-unit re-extraction reduced individual-level pseudoreplication. Historical screening records and independent review of new endpoint mappings remain incomplete.', 'Dose-adjusted analyses likewise provided no corrected evidence of stage separation.')
replace('Life-stage comparisons under',abstract)
replace('We conducted a secondary','We reanalysed a curated effect-size database derived substantially from the published synthesis of Alter et al. (2024), with a targeted update for studies published through 2025. The source synthesis reports Google Scholar and ISI Web of Science searches, annual Google Scholar retrievals for 2008–2022, reference tracing, and eligibility of factorial warming-by-acidification experiments. Its published methods and five-expert metric scores are archived with this revision. The inherited database, targeted-update logs and current record-level crosswalk have distinct provenance; the published PRISMA flow does not document a new search executed by this study (Page et al., 2021).')
replace('The analysis unit is','The analysis unit is an endpoint-specific treatment-control log response ratio; StudyID groups linked source records for dependence control. Thirty-two identical cross-layer pairs in studies 3, 10, 13 and 193 were reduced to one record per pair. A subsequent source audit restored 11 records across 10 groups in which identical rounded effects represented distinct exposure treatments. Dose variables and source locators now distinguish these records. The 32 incorrect original-workbook pointers were replaced by matching published-data locators; unverified original pointers are retained only as legacy audit fields. Equal effect values or unique RowKeys do not establish experimental independence. The source tables preserve all changes and the unmodified input.')
replace('Full-database descriptions use',f"Full-database descriptions use {len(d):,} effects from {d.StudyID.nunique()} studies. Primary stage analyses use {len(primary):,} effects from {primary.StudyID.nunique()} studies in taxa with a metamorphic stage. We use k for effects, m for studies and s for analysis-specific study cells. Source matching restored temperature, pCO2, pH-scale, acclimation and tank-count fields that had been dropped from the model table. Treatment temperature was available for {int(expo.TreatmentTemperature_C.notna().sum()):,} effects and treatment pCO2 for {int(expo.Treatment_pCO2_uatm.notna().sum()):,}. Acclimation was retained as a separate variable and was not substituted for exposure duration (Supplementary Table S3).")
replace('Raw directional response is','Directional response is lnRR = ln(treatment mean/control mean) for the represented endpoint. In the inherited source, mortality and abnormality measurements were converted to survival and morphological normality before effect-size calculation (Alter et al., 2024). These records already describe positive measurement conventions. We therefore use TransformedMetric to interpret the stored lnRR and do not reverse its sign again because the original Metric contains an adverse term. Other effects retain their recorded measurement direction. Sampling variances use the response-ratio approximation (Hedges et al., 1999; Lajeunesse, 2011), except for the design-aware re-extraction described below.')
replace('A deterministic dictionary','Endpoint alignment first matches the represented metric to the published five-expert consensus of Alter et al. (2024). A unanimous positive score retains the lnRR sign; a unanimous negative score reverses it. Published ambiguous or inconsistent scores are excluded. Explicit represented survival, normality and developmental-success endpoints without an exact score-table entry retain the direction of the source transformation. Other unmatched metrics are excluded. This rule avoids extending growth, calcification, timing or physiological proxies to fitness without a source justification. A sensitivity analysis further restricts eligibility to exact published-consensus matches. External expert scoring is attributed to its authors and is not described as new independent coding in this reanalysis.')
replace('The dictionary retains',f"The source-based rules retain {int(scope.iloc[2].k):,} effects from {int(scope.iloc[2].m)} studies overall and {int(scope.iloc[3].k):,} effects from {int(scope.iloc[3].m)} studies in the primary subset. For settlement/metamorphosis, {settle_text}. Supplementary Table S4 records original and represented metrics, published ratings, direction rules and exclusions. A second human review of this study's mappings has not been completed; the corresponding worksheet is supplied without fabricated agreement statistics.")
replace('For Figure 2,','For Figure 2, all three targets use identical eligible rows within each full-database subgroup. Directional and aligned effects are inverse-variance weighted within study-by-subgroup-by-driver cells. Aggregate variances propagate a working correlation of 0.8: v_cell = [0.2 sum(w) + 0.8 {sum(sqrt(w))} squared] / {sum(w)} squared, with w = 1/v. Absolute response is calculated after signed aggregation. Separate REML models use Hartung-Knapp confidence intervals and prediction intervals. The absolute summary uses the same working variance and is descriptive; no zero-null P value is assigned to folded responses. Signed-target P values are BH-adjusted within each moderator-by-target family across levels and drivers. Figure 3a uses the same working-variance and interval conventions.')
# Insert additional methods before Results.
i=lines.index('3. Results')
lines[i:i]=['2.7 Recovered exposure and experimental-unit analyses',
'For StudyID 243, publicly archived chemistry measurements were averaged across technical duplicates, aquarium-week samples and aquaria before assigning species-level treatment exposures. Final morphology measurements were averaged within aquarium before calculating means, standard deviations, sample sizes and lnRR; ten morphology effects were re-extracted. Behavioural individual identifiers could not be linked uniquely to aquaria and remain clustered at StudyID. The repository describes a 92-day manipulation. For StudyID 244, the full text distinguishes approximately eight months of experimental exposure from a study spanning nearly one year and a final calcification assay of approximately nine weeks; the Dryad description also contains an eight-week wording, retained in the source notes. StudyID 246 reports a 48-hour exposure.',
'StudyID 244 shares control treatments, coral genets, header tanks and mesocosms across measurements. We re-extracted 24 effects using equal-header means of observed tank means, rather than treating coral branches as independent replicates. A joint 9,999-draw procedure used mean-one exponential genet weights within species and collection site, shared across treatments, plus resampling of headers within treatments and tanks within headers. The same header/tank draws were used across species. Positive genet weights preserved sparse observed tank cells; missing outcomes were not imputed. The joint covariance of all 24 log ratios was archived and used in an explicit-covariance aggregation sensitivity. Only two headers per treatment were available, so we also excluded this study in a separate stage analysis.',
'Exploratory dose meta-regressions were fitted separately by driver and response definition, with correlated-effects RVE, StudyID clustering and small-sample corrections. Predictors were life stage, control temperature and the relevant temperature increment or log2(treatment/control pCO2); combined-exposure models included both increments. Models required a full-rank design, at least three studies per stage and ten studies per coefficient. Coefficients with fewer than four small-sample degrees of freedom were flagged as unstable. BH correction was applied to coefficient tests within each response definition and to the six adjusted stage-omnibus tests as a separate family. These observational adjustments do not isolate causal dose effects; pH scales were not combined, and sparse verified exposure durations were not meta-regressed (Supplementary Fig. S13; Supplementary Tables S14–S16).']
# Replace ecological conclusions by the actual moderator tests.
a=lines.index('3.2 Ecological heterogeneity in the full database');b=lines.index('3.2.1 Matched response definitions within subgroups')
section=['3.2 Ecological heterogeneity in the full database','All-endpoint moderator tests describe variation in available directional evidence. Matched-target forests hold endpoint eligibility constant within each subgroup (Fig. 2); these analyses use the full database.']
